{
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 160,
      "text": "Your privacy, you have to treat addresses as single-use. You should generate a brand-new address, a new invoice for every single"
    },
    {
      "id": 161,
      "text": "transaction. Absolutely. Furthermore, privacy"
    },
    {
      "id": 162,
      "text": "cryptocurrency is drastically reduced where the blockchain meets the traditional financial system. You mean like exchanges? Yes."
    },
    {
      "id": 163,
      "text": "When you use a corporate currency exchange to buy cryptocurrency with a traditional bank account, you have to pass Know Your Customer"
    },
    {
      "id": 164,
      "text": "or KYC checks. Right. You have to hand over your driver's license, your passport, your banking details."
    },
    {
      "id": 165,
      "text": "Exactly. And once the exchange links your real-world identity to your withdrawal address, your pseudonymous shield is completely"
    },
    {
      "id": 166,
      "text": "gone. Chain analysis companies can track your previous and future transactions across the public ledger. Wow."
    },
    {
      "id": 167,
      "text": "Okay, let's map all this theory out in real time. Let's walk through the actual step-by-step mechanics of a transaction. Okay, let's do"
    },
    {
      "id": 168,
      "text": "it. Say our fictional user Alice wants to receive a fraction of a bitcoin from her friend Joe. What"
    },
    {
      "id": 169,
      "text": "actually happens when she opens her phone? First, Alice opens her mobile non-custodial wallet. Got it."
    },
    {
      "id": 170,
      "text": "She hits the receive button. Her wallet uses her master seed phrase to mathematically derive a brand-new, never-before"
    },
    {
      "id": 171,
      "text": "used private key. For privacy? Exactly. And from that, it generates a brand-new public address. To make it"
    },
    {
      "id": 172,
      "text": "easy, the wallet displays this address as a QR code on her screen. Which is brilliant, because typing out a 35-character string of random case-"
    },
    {
      "id": 173,
      "text": "sensitive letters is a guaranteed recipe for a typo. It's just asking for a mistake. So Joe opens his wallet up, selects Send"
    },
    {
      "id": 174,
      "text": "and uses his phone's camera to scan her QR code. Right. And Joe wants to send her 0.01 bitcoin."
    },
    {
      "id": 175,
      "text": "Depending on the interface he uses, that might be displayed as 1 milli-bitcoin or 100,000 satoshis. Satoshis"
    },
    {
      "id": 176,
      "text": "being the absolute smallest fractional unit of a bitcoin, named after the creator, right? You got it. So Joe"
    },
    {
      "id": 177,
      "text": "enters the 100,000 satoshis. But before he sends, he has to set a transaction fee. Yes. And this isn't a flat"
    },
    {
      "id": 178,
      "text": "fee charged by a bank. How does it work then? He gets to choose the fee based on a free market. Every transaction"
    },
    {
      "id": 179,
      "text": "goes into a waiting room called the mempool."
    }
  ]
}
